data: {"type":"agent_chunk","agent":"researcher","content":"- CRISPR gene editing modifies the genomes of living organisms. It is based on a simplified version of the bacterial CRISPR-Cas9 antiviral defense system [1].\n- In the technique, the Cas9 nuclease is delivered into a cell with a synthetic guide RNA (gRNA), which cuts the genome at a desired location [1].\n- CRISPR is a family of DNA sequences in prokaryotes such as bacteria and archaea. The sequences come from bacteriophage DNA and help detect and destroy similar phages [2].\n- Gene editing makes targeted changes by inserting, deleting, or substituting DNA. CRISPR, zinc finger nucleases, and TALENs are examples [3].\n- A 2021 headline reports a CRISPR breakthrough that may open the door to treating many diseases [4]. A 2024 headline reports HIV in cell culture was eliminated using CRISPR-Cas editing [5]."}

