data: {"type":"agent_chunk","agent":"analyst","content":"- **Mechanism:** Cas9 nuclease paired with a synthetic guide RNA cuts DNA at a chosen site [1]. The system is adapted from bacterial antiviral defense, which uses phage-derived sequences to detect and destroy similar viruses [1][2].\n\n- **Broad editing toolkit:** Gene editing inserts, deletes, or substitutes DNA. CRISPR is one of several methods, alongside zinc finger nucleases and TALENs [3].\n\n- **Promising but early therapeutic results:** A 2021 headline suggested CRISPR could treat many diseases [4]. A 2024 report showed HIV eliminated in cell culture, which is lab-stage evidence rather than clinical proof [5]."}

